<h1>Orcraft Logger</h1>
<p>Most of an Orcraft log writes itself. Every component logs its config table when it starts and its
  result table when it finishes, nested by level, and every
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/statuses/#checkpoints">checkpoint</a> logs its
  outcome either way. <code>OrcraftLogger</code> is for everything a test wants to add on top of that.</p>

{{ codeblock.block('from orcraft import OrcraftLogger as Logger

Logger.log("Starting the run", color=Logger.Color.BLUE)') }}

<p>Importing it as <code>Logger</code> is the convention throughout Orcraft suites, and it is what keeps
  call sites short. Everything on it is a class method - there is no logger instance to create, pass
  around or configure.</p>

<h2 id="log">Messages</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Argument</th>
      <th>Default</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>*args</code></td>
      <td>-</td>
      <td>Values to log, joined with spaces, like <code>print</code>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>color</code></td>
      <td><code>DEFAULT</code></td>
      <td>Foreground color from <code>Logger.Color</code>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>bg_color</code></td>
      <td><code>DEFAULT</code></td>
      <td>Background color from <code>Logger.Background</code>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>timestamp</code></td>
      <td><code>False</code></td>
      <td>Append the time the line was logged.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>module_tag</code></td>
      <td><code>True</code></td>
      <td>Prefix the line with the calling module. Pass <code>False</code> for output that should read as the program's own, such as captured stdout.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>tab</code></td>
      <td><code>None</code></td>
      <td>Indent level, so a message nests with the component that emitted it.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

{{ codeblock.block('Logger.log(f"Setup: preparing {input_count} input files", color=Logger.Color.CYAN)
Logger.log(completed.stdout, module_tag=False)
Logger.log(completed.stderr, color=Logger.Color.RED, module_tag=False)') }}

<p><code>module_tag=False</code> is the one worth knowing about. Output captured from the system under
  test should appear as that program printed it; tagging it with the module that happened to relay it
  makes the log harder to read, not easier.</p>

<p><code>Logger.log_error</code> takes the same arguments, forces red and writes to stderr. It is for
  reporting a problem, not for causing one - the result status is set by
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/statuses/#checkpoints">checkpoints</a> and by the
  result descriptions, never by a log call.</p>

<h2 id="colors">Colors</h2>
<p><code>Logger.Color</code> covers the usual named colors and their light and dark variants, plus an
  eleven-step gray ramp and a nine-step brown ramp. Every member is listed with a swatch in
  <a class="text-accent" href="#appendix-colors">Appendix: color reference</a>.</p>

<p>To color part of a line rather than all of it, build the string with <code>colorize</code>:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('parts.append(Logger.colorize(segment, Logger.Color.GREEN))

status_text = Logger.colorize_if(
	label,
	passed,
	Logger.Color.GREEN,
	Logger.Color.RED,
)') }}

<p><code>colorize</code> returns the text wrapped in ANSI codes, so it composes into a larger message,
  into an f-string, or into a table cell. <code>colorize_if</code> is the two-way version that most
  status rendering ends up wanting.</p>

<p>The same mechanism drives result field
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/result-fields/#result-fields">formatters</a>: a
  formatter that returns a colorized string is how a value arrives in the result table already
  colored.</p>

<h2 id="tables">Tables</h2>
<p>The framework logs config and result tables on its own. When a test has tabular data of its own to
  report - a comparison, a per-item breakdown - it can log a table directly:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('Logger.log_table(
	headers=("Case", "Requested", "Measured", "Delta"),
	rows=rows,
	title="My Test Summary",
)') }}

<p>Columns are sized uniformly, the title is centered over them, and <code>column_align</code> and
  <code>column_widths</code> are there when the defaults do not suit. <code>tab</code> works as it does
  for <code>log</code>, so a table nests with the component that emitted it.</p>

<p>Whether the framework's own tables read horizontally or vertically is set per component with
  <code>log_orientation</code>. Horizontal is compact for a handful of fields; vertical stays readable
  when there are many, or when values are long:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('log_orientation = OrcraftConfigField.Format(
	default=OrcraftConfigField.LogTableOrientation.VERTICAL
)') }}

<h2 id="progress">Progress and inline output</h2>
<p>For work that takes long enough to need a sign of life, <code>log_inline</code> rewrites a single
  console line instead of scrolling:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('for index, item in enumerate(items, start=1):
	Logger.log_inline_progress("Processing", index, len(items))
	process(item)

Logger.log_inline_progress("Processing", len(items), len(items), finalize=True)') }}

<p>Call with <code>finalize=True</code> once at the end to terminate the line before other logs resume -
  without it the next message lands on top of the progress bar. <code>build_progress_bar</code> returns
  the bar and its fraction if you would rather compose the line yourself.</p>

<p>Inline output is a console convenience; the log file keeps the full record.</p>

<h2 id="log-files">Log files</h2>
<p>Console output is mirrored to a log file, with the ANSI decoration preserved - which is why a log
  opened later, or rendered by the controller, looks the way the run looked. The framework initializes
  the logger on first use, so a test never has to; <code>Logger.get_log_file_path()</code> returns the
  file currently being written when a test needs to reference or attach it.</p>

<h2 id="appendix-colors">Appendix: color reference</h2>
<p>Every member of <code>Logger.Color</code> is listed below. <code>Logger.Background</code> declares
  exactly the same set of names, so anything valid as a foreground color is valid as a background
  one:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('Logger.log(
	"Run finished",
	color=Logger.Color.WHITE,
	bg_color=Logger.Background.DARK_GREEN,
)') }}

<p>The swatches show how each color renders against a standard terminal palette. The 16-color members are
  resolved by whatever palette the terminal is configured with, so their exact appearance varies; the
  gray, brown and navy members are 256-color indices and are stable across terminals.</p>

